# What is a prime number?

{#org-slipshow-note-209}
> Open by asking who remembers the sieve of Eratosthenes.
>
> Do **not** get drawn into the twin prime conjecture here &mdash; it comes up
> again on the last slip.

Some motivating text before the definition.

{pause}

{.definition title="Prime number"}
A **prime number** is an integer divisible by exactly two integers: 1, and
itself.

Literal braces like \{this one\} must survive the trip intact.

## A nested heading

Inline math $e^{i\pi} = -1$, and display math:

$$\left( \sum_{k=1}^n a_k b_k \right)^2 < \Phi$$

{slip}
-----
# Properties

-   Every integer greater than 1 has a prime factor.
-   [There are infinitely many primes.]{pause}
-   [Primes thin out, but they never stop.]{pause}
-   [See [OEIS A000040](https://oeis.org/A000040) for the sequence itself.]{pause}

{.theorem title="Euclid"}
> There are infinitely many prime numbers.

{.proof}
> Suppose there were finitely many. Multiply them all together and add one.

{speaker-note pause}
> A second note in one section takes a step of its own, so the note can
> change partway through. Give them a moment with the proof first.

{slip}
